WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.400
What attracted me to Tessl was when I was first introduced

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.920
to the philosophy that, hey, these document specifications,

3
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:15.120
this plain text context is now going to drive the new age of programming.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.120
Something about that really resonated.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.720
It's been a really good experience.

6
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.720
The people here are lovely, and I think that that helps me work a little bit.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.240
I like being personable with other people.

8
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.400
I like talking to people.

9
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.400
I like coming into the office.

10
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.400
I like interacting with others.

11
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.920
the calibre of the team is very high.

12
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.320
So I think we have an incredibly rich and experienced team.

13
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.960
And something that really stood out to me was that considering a team

14
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.080
that has so much experience and has worked in many different fields of engineering

15
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.880
before, the amount of openness to change and the amount of willingness

16
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.360
to actually try new technologies and try different things, was really high.

17
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.880
And so we get not only the experience of some incredible engineers

18
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.080
that have worked on some complex systems before,

19
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.200
but then pair that with a willingness to try different things

20
00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.760
that pair really well with this environment right now.

21
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.920
The thing that, for me, has set the culture here

22
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.280
apart by far over other places that I've worked at

23
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.080
is a combination of the incredibly high trust and high autonomy.

24
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.800
I think that that very clearly sets the culture apart.

25
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.560
I don't have to seek any approval to actually go and work

26
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.880
on a solution to a problem that I think is genuinely meaningful, right?

27
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.960
If I see something myself and I have the time and the resources,

28
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.080
I can go and work on it myself, and the team is always receptive to these ideas.

29
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.680
We have multiple people that have shipped multiple things in the last few months

30
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.240
that were not on our roadmap,

31
00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.520
that came and were born out of an idea or intuition that they have.

32
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.280
the leadership have created a culture

33
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.120
where everyone's intuition is very clearly valued.

34
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.960
I would love to still be here in five years.

35
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.120
I see myself here growing a lot more into the sort of work that I want to do,

36
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.200
specifically from the perspective of product and engineering

37
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:09.400
and driving solutions to meaningful problems in our space.

38
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.280
Hopefully we've had a massive impact on our industry in five years.

39
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.360
That'd be awesome.
